as the school year winds down for many  students experts point to a growing  problem that could be worse by the fall  since the pandemic began chronic  absences in the u.s schools have doubled  pre-pandemic about 8 million students  nationwide were chronically absent now  it's up to around 16 million meg oliver  visited a harper school district in  connecticut which is taking bold action  to reduce absent rates  it was a sweet surprise for these  excited 6th graders at mcdonough middle  school after a scavenger hunt  ice cream sandwiches  [Applause]  and a dj dance party with their teachers  and friends to participate the students  had to be in school for 90 percent of  april this is just one of many events by  the district to encourage attendance  principal marjorie rice we've had fear  factor events where staff has actually  eaten  insects teachers are eating insects  insects to entice students exactly is it  working it's working yes the kids do  love the events our efforts have shown  in our data that  we're improving our chronic absenteeism  reducing that rate improving our average  daily attendance but most importantly  students who maybe were disengaged in  the beginning we're starting to see them  coming to school  every day the u.s department of  education defines chronic absences as  students who miss at least 15 days of  school in a year according to the  organization attendance works trauma  family responsibilities or home  situations health or transportation  problems poverty housing and food  insecurity all contribute to chronic  absences  are absences higher than ever absolutely  we are having an attendance crisis as a  country hedy chang is the executive  director and president of attendance  works she says schools need to make kids  feel connected and engaged offer  tutoring and mentoring expand summer  learning and do at home visits when kids  aren't showing up is it too late to take  action right now before the end of this  school year absolutely not it starts  with that outreach  we need to make sure that we're reaching  out to students and families that's  exactly what hartford public schools are  also doing when did the home visits  really ramp up  last week we did 15  and the week before that we did 15 as  well wow ashley jackson is the middle  school student engagement specialist  you're going to follow the deck in that  order she helps put on events like the  scavenger hunt but also visits families  at home when students aren't showing up  to school how many students have you  been able to re-engage  a lot  i can't even put a number on it 8th  grader ashley quadra is one of the  students jackson has helped during the  pandemic her family moved and she no  longer qualified for school  transportation  jackson taught her how to take the  public bus riding the route with her if  you didn't have that do you think there  might have been a day where you thought  this is too much this is too hard i  don't need to go to school today yes  really  quadra now reaches out to students who  are often absent what do you tell them  why should they come back  because you need education to get  further in life because when you get  older you're probably going to want to  have your career and you can't make a  career out of no education because that  education is going to get you far in  life when you see your students smiling  when you see them engaged when you see  them doing these scavenger hunts how  does it make you feel it's impossible to  put it into words  it is the best feeling in the world  it just fills me with joy it fills me  with a ton of pride and a whole lot of  hope  shortly after we finished filming at the  middle school there was a deadly  drive-by shooting across the street our  cameraman heard the shots the school  went into lockdown and no students or  staff were hurt violence has also been  cited as a factor to contributing to  absenteeism a school spokesperson told  us everyone was rattled but they're  determined to persevere despite the  challenges they faced growing up  surrounded by violence there you go  right no one was hurt meaning no one was  struck by a bullet but hearing the shots  that rattles you and that changes your  conception of school isn't it safe can i  learn here where am i going with this  and that young girl put it so perfectly  i mean this education this is where it  starts if you want to change  circumstance it starts with the  education at a young age they have to be  able to get there for that i think the  big takeaway too is it's not too late to  take action you know even after i did  the interview with hedy chang i wanted  to volunteer at my daughter's school and  to like reach out tutor a mentor i think  this is a community effort right now  everybody has to step up it takes a  village right absolutely and as long as  you understand like tony is saying these  things that the children are dealing  with whether it's violence or bullying  or even just difficulty of doing your  assignments as parents as teachers we  all can join in and kind of get these  kids re-engaged in the classroom worried  about public schools we've got teachers  not signing up for the job or leaving  the job and then we've got kids doubling  the chronic absent rate that's a big  issue maybe it's a call to all parents  out there volunteer a little bit more of  your time thank you man we appreciate  you
 

